public class EqualOperatorExample {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		String str1 = "Hello";
		String str2 = "Hello";
		// compare two identical strings using ==
		if(str1 == str2) {
			System.out.println("The two strings are equal");
		}
	}
}

In this example, both str1 and str2 point to the same memory location in the string pool, so they have the same equal values and the condition passes.

2. Using .equals() method
When should you use the .equals() method to compare strings?

You should use the .equals() method to compare strings when you want to check if the actual values of two strings are equal. Here is an example:

public class EqualsMethodExample {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		String str1 = "Hello";
		String str2 = "World";
		// compare two different strings using ==
		if(str1.equals(str2)) {
			System.out.println("The two strings are equal");
		}
	}
}

In this example, the strings are not equal, so the condition doesn't pass and the output does not print.

When not to use == operator?
You should not use the == operator to compare strings in the following situations:

- When comparing String objects that were created using the new keyword, because the strings may refer to different memory locations, and the comparison may not be accurate.
- When comparing String objects that are passed as parameters to methods, because the parameters may be passed by value, and the comparison may not be accurate.

In conclusion, the == operator should be used to compare strings only when you are sure that the strings are stored in the same memory location, and the .equals() method should be used to compare strings when you want to check if the actual values of the strings are equal.

